"Leadership: Language skills are essential for world citizens
Independent Schools in Scotland maintain a trajectory of academic excellence that has continued in 2018 with a series of outstanding results, which is reinforced with individual and collective success in sports, art, music and other community efforts.
With more than 30,000 students from all over Scotland, these schools, represented by the Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS), strive to provide the best service to the students and their parents.
The independent schools aim to prepare the students for subsequent and higher education, for the career they choose and for their place as citizens of the world.
As an educational sector that is able to design and implement a personalized school curriculum, we can see that modern languages continue to be a very popular and desired subject in schools.
Nelson Mandela said, “If you talk to a man in a language he understands, you’ll come into your head.
“If you speak it in your own language, you’ll come into your heart.”
It’s a very important reminder that we can’t just rely on English if we want to establish relationships and generate confidence with people from other countries.
Based on the recent results of this year’s exams, we can see that languages lead the ranking tables with the highest qualifications in independent schools.
Sixty-six percent of the students who studied foreign languages received a higher grade.
According to data collected from 74 schools, 72% of the students received a higher score, while 72% of German students, 69% of French students, and 63% of Spanish students also received an A.
This demonstrates that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages as vital competences that children and young people will undoubtedly need in the future.
At the same time, languages, such as STEM (Science, Technology, Engineering and Mathematics), are kept in the same position as STEM (science, technology, engineering and math) in the curricula of independent schools and in other places.
According to a survey carried out in 2014 by the Committee on Employment and Employment of the United Kingdom, it was revealed that 17% of the reasons for the lack of linguistic skills were attributed to the lack of language skills.
Therefore, linguistic competences are increasingly essential to prepare young people for their future careers.
Since there are more and more potential job opportunities that require languages, these competencies are essential in a globalised world.
Regardless of a person’s career, if he/she has learned a second language, he/she will have a real advantage in the future with a permanent competition like this.
The fact of being able to communicate directly with people from other countries automatically puts a multilingual person in front of the competition.
“According to a survey carried out in 2013, more than 4,000 adults in the United Kingdom did not know how to speak a foreign language in the United Kingdom, with 15 per cent of those who did not speak a foreign language in the United Kingdom.
For this reason, investing in language teaching is important for today’s children.
Knowing a variety of languages, especially those developed, will give children the best opportunities to find an important job.
In Scotland, there is diversity between the schools in the languages that are taught.
Some schools focus more on traditional modern languages, while others teach languages that are considered to be more important for the United Kingdom in the future, such as Mandarin or Japanese.
Regardless of your child’s interest, you will always be able to choose between a series of languages in independent schools, which have specialized teaching staff in this field.
The independent Scottish schools are dedicated to providing a learning environment that will prepare the children and provide them with the skills they need to be successful, regardless of their future.
In the global commercial environment of the current era, it is not possible to denounce that languages continue to play a vital importance for the future of the country, and this has to be reflected in education.
In fact, modern languages should really be regarded as “inter-communicative competences”.
The independent schools will continue to offer this option, diversity and excellence to young Scots.
It's a bien le faire.
John Edward is Director of the Scottish Council of Independent Schools
