The police told the investigators that he felt good about what he had done.
According to the indictment, Saipov explained to the authorities that he was inspired to carry out the attack after seeing ISIS videos on his phone.
According to the police, he was also asked to remove the ISIS flag in his hospital room.
He pleaded not guilty of the accusation of 22 offences.
David Patton, one of the federal defenders representing Saipov, stated that they were "obviously disillusioned" with the indictment.
“We believe that the decision to ask for the death penalty, instead of accepting a declaration of guilt and a life-threatening life-threatening act without the possibility of freedom will only prolong the trauma of these events for all those involved.”
Saipov's defense team had previously asked prosecutors not to demand the death penalty.
