Upgrading the red tide: the concentrations are reduced in Pinellas, Manatee and Sarasota
The Florida Fish and Wildlife Commission's latest report shows a general reduction in red tide concentrations in certain parts of the Tampa Bay area.
According to the FWC, more dispersed proliferation conditions are being observed in the areas of Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collier, which suggests a lower concentration.
The proliferation of the red tide stretches along about 200 km of coastline, from the north of Pinellas in the southern part of Lee.
You can find stains about 16 km from the Hillsborough coast, but in less places, compared to last week.
The red tide has also been observed in Pasco County.
Average concentrations have been reported on the coast or at a distance from the coast in the County of Pinellas over the course of the last week, low concentrations at high altitudes in the County of Hillsborough, high concentrations in the County of Manatee, high concentrations in the County of Charlotte, concentrations at the bottom of the coast or at a distance of the County of Lee County and low concentrations in the County of Collier.
Respiratory irritations are still reported in the counties of Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier.
There has not been reported respiratory irritation over the past week in the north-east of Florida.
